<!-- doc/src/sgml/oracle-pipelined.sgml -->

<chapter id="oracle-pipelined">
  <title>Oracle Pipelined Table Functions</title>

 <indexterm zone="oracle-pipelined">
  <primary>pipelined table function</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <para>
  A <firstterm>pipelined table function</firstterm> returns its result as a
  stream of rows instead of materializing the whole collection first.  Each
  <literal>PIPE ROW</literal> statement hands one row to the caller and
  suspends the function until the caller asks for the next row, so a query
  that stops early (for example <literal>LIMIT 10</literal>) never executes
  the remainder of the function.  This mirrors the behavior of the same
  feature in Oracle databases and is available on a server initialized in
  Oracle compatibility mode.
 </para>

 <sect1 id="oracle-pipelined-creating">
  <title>Creating a Pipelined Function</title>

  <para>
   A pipelined function returns a collection type declared with
   <literal>CREATE TYPE ... AS TABLE OF</literal>:
<programlisting><![CDATA[
CREATE TYPE num_tab AS TABLE OF NUMBER;

CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION f_num_list(p_n NUMBER)
RETURN num_tab PIPELINED IS
BEGIN
  FOR i IN 1 .. p_n LOOP
    PIPE ROW(i);
  END LOOP;
  RETURN;
END;
/]]></programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The <literal>PIPELINED</literal> marker changes the meaning of the
   <literal>RETURN</literal> clause: it names the collection type whose
   <firstterm>element type</firstterm> the function pipes one row at a
   time.  Inside the body, <literal>PIPE ROW(<replaceable>expr</replaceable>)</literal>
   evaluates <replaceable>expr</replaceable> once, converts it to the
   element type, and delivers it to the caller as the next row.  A bare
   <literal>RETURN</literal> ends the stream.  Falling off the end of the
   function without a <literal>RETURN</literal> raises the usual
   <quote>control reached end of function without RETURN</quote> error,
   and <literal>PIPE ROW(NULL)</literal> pipes a genuine SQL NULL element
   rather than ending the stream.
  </para>

  <para>
   The element type may be a composite type, in which case each
   <literal>PIPE ROW</literal> produces a full row:
<programlisting><![CDATA[
CREATE TYPE emp_tab AS TABLE OF emp%ROWTYPE;]]></programlisting>
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="oracle-pipelined-consuming">
  <title>Consuming a Pipelined Function</title>

  <para>
   Call a pipelined function in the <literal>FROM</literal> clause wrapped
   in <literal>TABLE(...)</literal>, exactly like a collection:
<programlisting><![CDATA[
SELECT column_value FROM TABLE(f_num_list(3));
 column_value
--------------
            1
            2
            3

SELECT e.ename FROM TABLE(emp_by_dept(20)) e;]]></programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Pipelined functions are planned as ordinary set-returning functions and
   stream lazily.  Side effects observe the on-demand behavior:
<programlisting><![CDATA[
CREATE SEQUENCE s;

CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION f_counted(p_n NUMBER)
RETURN num_tab PIPELINED IS
BEGIN
  FOR i IN 1 .. p_n LOOP
    PERFORM nextval('s');
    PIPE ROW(i);
  END LOOP;
  RETURN;
END;
/

SELECT setval('s', 1, false);
SELECT column_value FROM TABLE(f_counted(100)) LIMIT 1;
-- the sequence has been called exactly once, not 100 times
SELECT currval('s');]]></programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Multiple invocations in one query keep independent execution state, and
   the functions can be used in joins, subqueries, and
   <literal>LATERAL</literal> correlation like any other row source.
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="oracle-pipelined-limits">
  <title>Restrictions</title>

  <para>
   The first release of this feature rejects the following forms with an
   <literal>0A000</literal> (<literal>feature_not_supported</literal>)
   error; they are candidates for later work:
   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <literal>VARRAY</literal> types and collections declared inside
      packages as the return type of a standalone pipelined function.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Polymorphic element types (<literal>ANYELEMENT</literal>,
      <literal>RECORD</literal>, and friends).
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Collection methods (<literal>EXTEND</literal>, <literal>COUNT</literal>,
      ...) inside the function body.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Parallel execution: a pipelined function is marked
      <literal>PARALLEL UNSAFE</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="oracle-pipelined-tools">
  <title>Tools and Dump/Restore</title>

  <para>
   <function>pg_get_functiondef</function> (and <application>psql</application>'s
   <command>\ef</command>) render a pipelined function with its original
   Oracle declaration, including the collection type and the
   <literal>PIPELINED</literal> marker, so the output can be replayed
   unchanged.
  </para>
  <para>
   <application>pg_dump</application> emits nested-table collection types
   as <literal>CREATE TYPE ... AS TABLE OF</literal> rather than as the
   underlying domain, and preserves the <literal>PIPELINED</literal>
   declaration of functions.  A dump taken from an Oracle-mode cluster
   should be restored into an Oracle-mode cluster, for example with
   <command>pg_restore</command> for archive-format dumps.
  </para>
 </sect1>
</chapter>
